#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""
jen-kea-helper — the fixed-function privilege boundary on a Kea host
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
v5.11.0. Installed at /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper, owned root:root
mode 0755. Jen (running as www-data on a *different* host) invokes it
over SSH as:

    sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper <op>

with ONE JSON object on stdin and gets ONE JSON object back on stdout.
Nothing else is ever written to stdout; a single diagnostic line goes
to stderr per call.

Why this exists
───────────────
Before 5.11.0 every Kea-side operation Jen performs — editing a subnet,
authoring a config, restarting a daemon, reading a log, installing a
package — was done by generating a Python script on the Jen host,
base64-ing it, piping it over SSH into `sudo python3`, and running it
as root. The documented Kea-side sudoers grant was therefore
`NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/python3`, i.e. **root**. A compromised www-data on
the Jen host was root on every Kea host it manages.

This helper replaces that with a closed set of operations behind ONE
sudoers line:

    youruser ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper

The bare command (no argument list) means "any arguments allowed" —
that is deliberate: the control is *this file's* op allowlist and path
walls, not sudo's argument matching. www-data cannot read or modify
this file, and the helper never executes anything it is handed: stdin
is data only. There is still no `self-update` op in the sense of
"install whatever Jen sends" — that capability is exactly what would
put "Jen writes a root-executed file on the Kea host" straight back.
From v6 (v5.66.0, Q103) there IS an `update` op, but it installs a
candidate ONLY when `ssh-keygen -Y verify` accepts a signature from the
Jen *project's* release key (never Jen's own SSH identity) under a
dedicated namespace, and only when the candidate declares a strictly
higher `HELPER_VERSION` — see that op's own section below. A helper
below v6 has no such op; updating it still needs a manual copy by an
administrator through the legacy grant, for that one last hop; see
docs/admin-guide.md "Kea host helper".

The legacy `sudo python3` path stays in Jen as a banner-warned fallback
for hosts that don't have the helper yet, and is never removed in the
5.x line (removing it would break a clean upgrade → a MAJOR change).

Protocol
────────
- stdin: one JSON object (an empty object is allowed). > 4 MiB → refused.
- stdout: exactly one JSON object, always with an `"ok"` boolean.
- exit 0 when the protocol worked (even if `"ok": false`, e.g. a config
  that failed `kea-dhcp4 -t`); exit 2 for protocol misuse (unknown op,
  unreadable JSON, oversize stdin) — still printing a JSON object.
- `HELPER_VERSION` is the protocol version. Bump it only when an op's
  request/response contract changes; Jen refuses a helper older than
  its `JEN_HELPER_MIN_VERSION`. `HELPER_BUILD` (v7, Q104) is a separate,
  plain integer bumped on EVERY change to this file, never reset by a
  version bump — `HELPER_VERSION` alone can't order two files that
  changed without a protocol change between them.

v2 (v5.16.0) — optimistic concurrency:
- `read-config` responses gain `"sha256"` — the hex SHA-256 of the raw
  file *bytes* (whitespace included), so Jen can tell if the file was
  hand-edited since a form was opened.
- `apply-config` accepts an optional `"expect_sha256"`. When present the
  op takes an exclusive `flock` on `<path>.jen_lock` (held through the
  replace), compares the current file's SHA against it BEFORE running
  `kea-dhcpX -t`, and returns `{"ok": false, "error": "conflict",
  "sha256": <current>}` on a mismatch — nothing written. `""` means
  "must not exist". Every `apply-config` response now carries the SHA of
  the bytes it just wrote.
- Every response envelope carries `"helper_version"`.

v3 (v5.23.0) — `"d2"` (kea-dhcp-ddns, Kea's DNS-update daemon) joins
`"dhcp4"`/`"dhcp6"` as a valid `service` everywhere one is accepted:
`read-config`/`test-config`/`apply-config` resolve it to the
`kea-dhcp-ddns` binary and (Jen-side) `kea-dhcp-ddns.conf`; `service`
resolves its unit to `kea-dhcp-ddns-server` / `isc-kea-dhcp-ddns-server`.
No protocol shape changed — a v2 caller talking to a v3 helper sees
identical behavior for dhcp4/dhcp6; a v2 helper simply refuses `"d2"`
as `not-allowed`, since `_valid_service` on that host doesn't know it
yet, which is why this is a version bump and not silently backward
compatible for the new capability.

v4 (v5.29.0) — `install-tls`: writes the TLS material for one daemon's
https control socket — exactly three files, `ca.crt`, `server.crt`,
`server.key`, under the FIXED directory `/etc/kea/tls/<service>/`. The
payload names the service and carries the three PEM bodies; it never
carries a path. The directory is created `root:root 0755`; the files are
written tmp + `os.replace`, owned `root:<daemon group>` (the unit's
`User=`, else `_kea`, else root), mode `0640` for the key and `0644` for
the certificates; a pre-planted symlink at the root, the directory, or
any target is refused. Content is guarded only by PEM block markers and
a 64 KiB cap — the helper is pure stdlib and cannot parse PEM; Jen
validates the material it generated before sending it. A v3 helper
answers `unknown-op`, which Jen turns into "needs helper v4"; the op is
never routed through the legacy `sudo python3` engine.

v5 (v5.49.0) — `tail-log` reads the file through a bounded
`collections.deque(f, maxlen=lines)` instead of `f.read().splitlines()`:
memory is bounded by the requested (at most 1000) lines whatever the size of
the log. The request and response shapes are unchanged; the bump exists so
Jen can offer the update (Settings → Kea → SSH shows "v5 available").

v6 (v5.66.0, Q103) — `update`: the ONE way this helper ever replaces itself.
A maintainer report: every helper update needed the legacy `NOPASSWD:
/usr/bin/python3` grant (real root) added by hand for one click, then
removed again — real friction, and the exact capability §3.3 exists to deny.
This closes it WITHOUT reopening "Jen writes a file the Kea host runs as
root": the candidate is installed only when it carries a valid
`ssh-keygen -Y verify` signature from the Jen project's own release key
(under a namespace, `jen-kea-helper`, distinct from the release-tarball
namespace `jen-release` — a checksum signature can never be replayed here or
vice versa) AND declares a STRICTLY higher `HELPER_VERSION` than this one.
A fully-compromised Jen can therefore install a genuine, newer Jen release's
helper and nothing else — the same trust root as the self-updater
(docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3.1, §3.3). The op never touches sudoers (the
helper's own sudoers line is unaffected either way) and never restarts
anything. `RELEASE_SIGNERS` here is byte-identical to the same-named
constant in `jen-update-root.py` (a test enforces it) — the local
`/etc/jen-kea-helper/allowed_signers` file (root-owned, not group/other
writable, ≤ 8 KiB) is an OPTIONAL addition on top of it, for key rotation
and test doubles, never a replacement.

v7 (v5.66.0-beta.2, Q104) — a third-party review of the v6 design (verdict:
"fundamentally good and should remain") found the hardening below still
missing. None of it changes what `update` VERIFIES — only how carefully
everything around it behaves:
- **No PATH trust.** Every binary this helper ever runs (`kea-dhcp4/6`,
  `kea-dhcp-ddns`, `systemctl`, `apt-get`, `ssh-keygen`) is resolved through
  `_find_bin()`, a root-owned allowlist walk over `_BIN_DIRS` — never
  `shutil.which()`, never a bare name handed to `subprocess.run`. The
  process itself now runs `#!/usr/bin/python3 -I` (isolated mode: no
  `PYTHON*` env vars, no script directory on `sys.path`) and every
  subprocess it spawns gets a fixed, minimal environment. `sudo -n
  /usr/local/sbin/jen-kea-helper` means the kernel resolves the shebang and
  every child process through sudo's own `secure_path` today, and that is
  genuinely safe on the targets this ships to — but a root privilege
  boundary must not depend on the caller's sudoers configuration staying
  that way forever.
- **`HELPER_BUILD`.** `HELPER_VERSION` is the protocol/capability number,
  bumped only when a request/response contract changes — but a helper-only
  fix (like the PATH hardening above) changes the FILE without changing
  the protocol, and `op_update`'s old `new_version <= HELPER_VERSION` check
  would call that `not-newer` forever. `HELPER_BUILD` is a plain integer,
  bumped on every change to this file, never reset by a version bump;
  `update` (from v7 on) requires the candidate's `HELPER_BUILD` to be
  greater than this file's own (equal or lower → `not-newer`), and its
  `HELPER_VERSION` to be at least this file's own (a protocol DOWNGRADE is
  still never installed, even signed, even with a higher build).
- **Preflight and rollback.** A signed, strictly-newer candidate could
  still have a syntax error, a Python-version incompatibility, or a
  host-specific import failure — `update` used to write and `os.replace`
  it with no check beyond "the bytes verify". Now, after the signature and
  version/build checks: the candidate is preflighted by running its own
  `version` op in a throwaway process (`/usr/bin/python3 -I <candidate>
  version` — the same fixed interpreter path every target has, exactly the
  path the legacy grant names — with an empty JSON object on stdin, a
  10 s timeout, the same fixed environment) and requiring exit 0 plus a
  JSON reply whose `helper_version`/`helper_build` match what the
  candidate declared; only then is it fsynced, the CURRENT helper copied
  aside to `<_SELF_PATH>.prev`, and `os.replace()`d in. A POSTFLIGHT repeats
  the same check against the newly-installed path — a failure there
  restores `.prev` over `_SELF_PATH` and reports `postflight-failed`
  instead of leaving a broken helper live; success removes `.prev`.

Build 8 (v5.66.0-beta.4, Q106) — a third-party review of v7 found the one
path through `op_update` that installs bytes with no way back: with no
regular file at `_SELF_PATH` (a rare state — the running helper normally
IS that file — but the postflight rollback above only fires `if
prev_saved`, and `prev_saved` was False here), the old code still ran the
replace, so a postflight failure on that first "update" had nothing to
restore. `update` replaces an installed helper, it never installs one:
`op_update` now refuses BEFORE writing or preflighting any candidate
bytes, with `not-installed`, whenever `_SELF_PATH` is not a plain regular
file. That makes the `.prev` copy unconditional — `prev_saved` is gone as
a variable — so a rollback is always attempted on a postflight failure,
and a rollback that ITSELF fails (`os.replace` raising, e.g. disk full)
is reported as `rollback-failed`, naming both `_SELF_PATH` and its
`.prev`, instead of the misleading `postflight-failed` (which implies the
old helper is back). This is the first build-only change: `HELPER_BUILD`
moves to 8 with `HELPER_VERSION` unchanged at 7 — the two new error
tokens are values inside the existing envelope, not a contract change.

Build 9 (v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109) — ChatGPT's review found a real gap in the ordering check
itself: `op_update` refused a candidate as `not-newer` only when `new_version <
HELPER_VERSION`, or (nested under `new_version == HELPER_VERSION`) when `new_build <=
HELPER_BUILD` — so a candidate declaring a HIGHER `HELPER_VERSION` skipped the build check
entirely, letting a lower build through on a protocol bump (a build-only fix accidentally
regressed by whatever built the higher-versioned candidate). The two checks are now
INDEPENDENT, run unconditionally in this order: `new_version < HELPER_VERSION` refuses with
detail `protocol-downgrade`; `new_build <= HELPER_BUILD` refuses with detail
`build-not-newer` — regardless of how the versions compare. `kea_host.py`'s own
`install_helper()` "already installed" short-circuit follows the identical rule now (it used
to compare `(version, build)` as a single lexicographic tuple, which had the same gap).
`_find_bin()` also gained a directory-level check, `_bin_dir_ok()`: the DIRECTORY a trusted
binary lives in (its `realpath`, so a usrmerge `/bin -> /usr/bin` symlink is followed) must
itself be root-owned and not group/other-writable before any file inside it is even looked
at — defence in depth alongside the existing per-file `_bin_owner_ok()` check.

Build 10 (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141) — the root:root rule of `_bin_owner_ok()` was applied to the
Kea DAEMON binary too, and ISC's own packages do not ship it that way: on a Kea 3.0.4 ISC
deb (`3.0.4 isc20260728182757 deb`) `/usr/sbin/kea-dhcp4` is `_kea:_kea`, mode 0750. Every op
that validates a config (`test-config` and `apply-config` both go through `_run_kea_test`)
answered `missingbinary` there, which Jen worded "kea-dhcp4 is not installed on this server" -
so no config change reached Kea through the helper from build 7 (v5.66.0-beta.2) until this
build. The test is now run AS THE ACCOUNT THE DAEMON RUNS AS, and trust follows who executes:
a binary the helper runs as root must still be root:root with no group/other write bit
(nothing else this helper runs changes); the Kea daemon binary is run through
`subprocess.run(..., user=, group=)` as the unit's `User=`, else the binary's own owner, and
is trusted only when it is a regular file owned by exactly that system account (a uid below
1000, never root) with no group/other write bit - what the daemon itself does on every start,
so a compromised `_kea`-owned binary gains nothing it did not already have, and the helper
(root) never executes it. Anything else is still `missingbinary`, now with the reason in
`detail` ("owned by alice, not the daemon's user"). The temp file `-t` reads is written 0644
explicitly (it used to rely on the umask) and `_CLEAN_ENV` gains `HOME=/` for the
dropped-privilege run. A change of behaviour inside the existing envelope, not a contract
change: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.

Build 11 (v5.68.0-beta.13, Q148) - two things build 10 got only partly right about the validation run. (1) The temp copy of the WHOLE
Kea config that `-t` reads carries the database credentials and was written 0644: whether another local account could reach it
depended on `/etc/kea`'s mode on the package in use, which is not something to depend on. It now takes the minimum the validator needs:
created with `os.open(..., 0o600)` and `fchown`/`fchmod` on the descriptor, owned by the account that runs `-t` (the daemon's own, when
it is run as that account) or by root (when it is run as root), 0600 either way, and removed on every exit path as before. (2) The run
used the daemon account's primary group and no supplementary groups, so a unit with `Group=` or `SupplementaryGroups=` (TLS material
readable through a group) started fine under systemd and failed Jen's `-t`. `_unit_account` now reads `User`, `Group` and
`SupplementaryGroups` in one `systemctl show`; the gid is the unit's `Group=` when set (else the passwd primary), `extra_groups` are the
unit's supplementary gids (resolved with `grp.getgrnam`), and a group the host does not have is `missingbinary` WITH the reason - never
a guess. A change of behaviour inside the existing envelope: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.

Build 12 (v5.68.0-beta.14, Q149) - build 11 consulted the unit's identity only AFTER it had decided the binary was not root:root, so a
root-owned binary under a unit that says `User=_kea` (a layout some packages and every hand install produce) was run as ROOT, and a unit
whose `User=` this host cannot resolve fell through to root as well. The order is now: (1) the unit's identity is resolved FIRST - `User=`
by name or by numeric uid, `Group=`, `SupplementaryGroups=`; (2) the binary is verified SECOND - root:root with no group/other write bit
(any unit), or owned by exactly the unit's own account (a system account, no group/other write bit); (3) `-t` runs as the unit's
identity whenever the unit names one, and as root only when it names none (or names root). A `User=` or `Group=` the host cannot resolve
is a refusal (`missingbinary` WITH the reason), never a run as root. The supplementary groups are what systemd itself gives the daemon:
`os.getgrouplist(name, gid)` (the account's /etc/group memberships) plus the unit's `SupplementaryGroups=`. A change of behaviour inside
the existing envelope: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.

Build 13 (v5.68.0-beta.15, Q150) - every file the helper writes is private from its FIRST BYTE, the lock is not optional, and a daemon-owned
binary must be executable by its owner. (1) `_private_tempfile(directory, prefix)` is the ONE way a file is created: a unique name in the
TARGET'S OWN directory (so two helper processes never share a path), `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW`, mode 0600 from the first byte; the
data is written and fsynced, the FINAL owner/group/mode is applied to the DESCRIPTOR (`fchown`/`fchmod`, never to a path another process
could swap), and the file is `os.replace`d into place. It replaces three writers that created a fixed-name file with the umask and fixed
it afterwards: `apply-config` (`<conf>.jen_apply_tmp`: the whole candidate config, database passwords included, 0644 until the mode was
copied), `install-tls` (`server.key`: the private key was written 0644 and chmod'ed 0640 after it was on disk) and the `.jen_backup` copy.
A replacement of a 0600 config is therefore never readable by another uid at any instant; the new-file mode (0644) and the key's final
0640 are unchanged, reached only once the file is complete. (2) The validation copy `-t` reads is `root:<the daemon's effective gid>`
0640 - READABLE by the account that runs the check (its `Group=` or primary group) but not WRITABLE by it: it used to be owned by that
account, so between the write and the `-t` it could change the very config it was about to validate. A root-run check keeps a root 0600
copy. (3) The `<conf>.jen_lock` flock is taken for EVERY `test-config`, `apply-config` and `install-tls` (it was taken only when an
`expect_sha256` was given), so a validation never races an apply of the same path and two `test-config`s never share a temp name; the
`expect_sha256` comparison stays the optional guard it is. (4) `_daemon_bin_ok` requires `S_IXUSR`: `os.access(X_OK)` is ROOT's answer,
true when ANY class may execute, so a `_kea`-owned file with `o+x` and no `u+x` passed and then failed to exec as `_kea`; a root-run
`root:root` binary needs its owner bit for the same reason, and one run as the unit's account needs the group/other bit it will use.
(5) A new op, `remove-config`: it removes a config file only if it still hashes to exactly the `expect_sha256` given, under the same lock
- the rollback of an Author Kea Config target that had no file before (Jen removes the file it created). A change of behaviour inside the
existing envelope plus one op: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.

Build 14 (v5.68.0-beta.16, Q151) - the identity, ownership and set-commit promises build 13 made, kept by EVERY path. (1) A brand-new Kea config is
owned `root:<the daemon's effective gid>` 0640 when the unit names a non-root account, `root:root` 0600 when the daemon runs as root - the identity
from `_unit_account`, never 0644 (it carries `lease-database.password`; build 13 created it 0644 so `_kea` could read it, and so could every local
account). (2) ONE identity resolver: `_daemon_group` (the unit's `User=` -> that account's passwd primary group, else `_kea`, else root) is deleted;
`install-tls` takes `server.key`'s group from `_unit_account` too (the effective gid after `Group=`) - a unit with `Group=kea-config` used to get a key its
daemon could not read, because the key's group came from one resolver and what validation ran as from the other. (3) `_finish_private` no longer swallows
a failed `fchown`: a requested ownership that cannot be applied ABORTS before the replace (the temp is removed, the live file untouched) instead of
installing a config or key owned by the wrong account and reporting success; the same for every other chown/fsync the helper does (`_chown`, the
swallowing wrapper, is gone; the one filesystem-cannot-fsync-a-directory case is named by errno, not by `except OSError: pass`). (4) `install-tls` commits
its three files as a SET (`_commit_file_set`): every member is staged private under a unique name, every live member is read into memory WITHOUT being moved,
each is then replaced, and on any failure every member already replaced is put back byte-for-byte (a member that did not exist is removed) - a failure at
any step used to leave a new `server.key` beside an old `server.crt`. A change of behaviour inside the existing envelope: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.

Build 17 (v5.68.0-beta.31, Q168) - the host's evidence is the daemon's own ANSWER. Build 16 treated "Kea logged that it started reloading, and nothing failed for three seconds" as a completed
reload; the completion line is INFO and a restored WARN level hides it, so a reload that never took was recorded as a restore. A restore is now recorded as done on exactly one of three
pieces of evidence, preferred in this order, and the state says which (`evidence`): `config-get` - the RUNNING daemon, asked on its own control socket (unix, or plain local http), shows
the logger the restored FILE has; `log` - Kea's own completion id past the pre-signal position, read incrementally and across rotation, only when no socket answered; `process` - after the
ONE restart, a NEW active main process free of a start-failure id. A reload-start line is never evidence. An existing state file that cannot be read is never "no session": arm, disarm,
status and the tick give one answer (`bad-state`). Every record is verified by this one contract, whatever build armed it (no legacy branch), the state is re-read under the lock (two
restores at once cannot spend the one restart), the target is the FILE the helper wrote, and a Kea that logs to syslog but has a local control socket can have investigation logging.

Build 16 (v5.68.0-beta.30, Q167) - the host's promise made provable. (1) A restore is recorded as done (`restored_at`) only when Kea's OWN log, past the offset noted before the
SIGHUP, shows the reload completed (`DHCP4_DYNAMIC_RECONFIGURATION_SUCCESS`) - or, when the restored level hides that INFO line, shows it STARTED (`DHCP4_DYNAMIC_RECONFIGURATION`) and no
failure id within a settle window; `unit is active` was never evidence that Kea re-read anything. A refused reload is reported and kept pending (the file is the problem), an unconfirmed one
gets the one restart and the same check; every failed tick counts (`attempts`) and ten of them set `needs_hand`. The arm records the Kea log path for this. (2) `investigation-arm`
never overwrites an unresolved record: an identical re-arm is idempotent, the same restore with a later deadline is an extension, anything else answers `armed` with the record. (3)
`investigation-status` reports the timer's own state (`timer_active`, `timer_enabled`) and a new op `investigation-timer` re-asserts it. (4) `--self-restore` exits 1 when a restore failed.

Build 15 (v5.68.0-beta.29, Q165) - the Kea HOST owns the restore of investigation logging. Eight reviews found a sequence after which Kea sat at DEBUG 55 and nothing
restored it, because the restore lived on the wrong machine and depended on Jen, Jen's database, Jen's config, the Control Agent and a marker in Kea's file. The DEBUG is
HERE and this helper already runs here as root. Three ops and one argv mode: `investigation-arm {service: "dhcp4", path, until, restore}` writes
`/var/lib/jen-kea-helper/investigation-dhcp4.json` (root 0600 in a root 0700 directory) and installs the timer (below); `investigation-disarm {service, path}` runs the
restore NOW; `investigation-status {service}` reads the state; `jen-kea-helper --self-restore [--now]` (argv, no stdin) restores every armed state whose `until` has
passed (every armed state with `--now`). The restore reads the config, puts the `kea-dhcp4` logger back from the STATE's `restore` object (never from the marker, which a
person or another tool can damage), deletes the marker, validates with `-t` as the daemon, writes the file privately, and asks the daemon to re-read it with SIGHUP (a restart
- at most ONE per state - when the unit is not active afterwards). It needs no Jen, no database, no API and no marker. The timer is a systemd oneshot service + timer
(`jen-kea-investigation.*`, boot + every 60 s) written by `investigation-arm` itself, running this file as root: NO sudoers change (the one-line grant is untouched). A host with
no systemd reports `timer: "none"` and Jen refuses to turn logging on there. `tests/vectors/investigation_restore.json` is the logger transformation both this file and
`kea_config_edit.clear_investigation_logging` are tested against. A change of behaviour plus ops inside the existing envelope: `HELPER_VERSION` stays 7.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import base64
import binascii
import collections
import contextlib
import copy
import errno
import hashlib
import json
import os
import posixpath
import re
import secrets
import shutil
import signal
import socket
import stat
import subprocess  # nosec B404 — every call is a fixed argv list, shell=False
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone

HELPER_VERSION = 7
HELPER_BUILD = 17
# v5.66.0-beta.2 (Q104) — HELPER_BUILD is bumped on EVERY change to this file, never by
# HELPER_VERSION alone; op_update's own _HELPER_BUILD_RE parses "^HELPER_BUILD = (\d+)$" out
# of a candidate's raw bytes, so this line (like HELPER_VERSION's own, just above) must never
# carry a trailing comment — anything after the digit breaks that end-of-line anchor and a
# genuine candidate would come back "unparseable".

MAX_STDIN = 4 * 1024 * 1024  # 4 MiB

# v5.66.0-beta.2 (Q104) — the root-owned binary allowlist every subprocess call resolves
# through, in this fixed order, NEVER $PATH. os.environ["PATH"] is under sudo's control
# today (Ubuntu's own secure_path), which is safe now but not something a root privilege
# boundary should depend on staying true. /usr/local/bin is where the system suite's
# systemctl/keactl stand-ins live (tests/system/compose/kea-node/Dockerfile).
_BIN_DIRS = ("/usr/sbin", "/usr/bin", "/sbin", "/bin", "/usr/local/sbin", "/usr/local/bin")
# The fixed environment every resolved binary runs under — never the inherited one. Ops
# that need an extra variable (apt-get's DEBIAN_FRONTEND) add it on top of this, per call.
_CLEAN_ENV = {"PATH": "/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8", "HOME": "/"}

# v4 — where `install-tls` writes, and the only names it writes. Fixed
# here, never taken from the payload: the op's whole path wall is "these
# three basenames under this one directory per service".
_TLS_ROOT = "/etc/kea/tls"
_TLS_FILES = ("ca.crt", "server.crt", "server.key")
_PEM_MAX = 64 * 1024
_PEM_CERTS_RE = re.compile(
    r"\A(?:-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\r?\n[A-Za-z0-9+/=\r\n]+-----END CERTIFICATE-----\r?\n?)+\Z"
)
_PEM_KEY_RE = re.compile(
    r"\A-----BEGIN (?:EC |RSA )?PRIVATE KEY-----\r?\n[A-Za-z0-9+/=\r\n]+-----END (?:EC |RSA )?PRIVATE KEY-----\r?\n?\Z"
)

# Directories a Kea config file may live in. Anything else is refused,
# independent of whatever path Jen sent — Jen validates its own copy,
# but this helper does not trust that.
_ALLOWED_CONF_DIRS = ("/etc/kea", "/usr/local/etc/kea")
_CONF_BASENAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\.conf$")

_ALLOWED_SERVICES = ("dhcp4", "dhcp6", "d2")

# service -> (binary name, systemd unit family). d2's unit family is
# "dhcp-ddns", not "d2" — Kea's own package/unit naming, not Jen's service
# vocabulary.
_SERVICE_BINARY = {"dhcp4": "kea-dhcp4", "dhcp6": "kea-dhcp6", "d2": "kea-dhcp-ddns"}
_SERVICE_UNIT_FAM = {"dhcp4": "dhcp4", "dhcp6": "dhcp6", "d2": "dhcp-ddns"}


# ── Path walls ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def _allowed_conf_path(p):
    """True iff `p` is an absolute, already-normalised path to a
    `*.conf` file directly inside one of the allowed Kea config dirs.
    posixpath, not os.path — these are always remote Linux paths."""
    if not isinstance(p, str) or not p:
        return False
    if not p.startswith("/") or posixpath.normpath(p) != p:
        return False
    if ".." in p.split("/"):
        return False
    if posixpath.dirname(p) not in _ALLOWED_CONF_DIRS:
        return False
    return bool(_CONF_BASENAME_RE.match(posixpath.basename(p)))


def _allowed_log_path(p):
    """True iff `p` is an absolute, already-normalised `*.log` path under
    /var/log (with symlinks in the existing prefix resolved)."""
    if not isinstance(p, str) or not p:
        return False
    if not p.startswith("/") or posixpath.normpath(p) != p:
        return False
    if not p.endswith(".log"):
        return False
    real = os.path.realpath(p)
    return real == "/var/log" or real.startswith("/var/log/")


def _valid_service(service):
    return service in _ALLOWED_SERVICES


def _file_sha256(path):
    """Hex SHA-256 of a file's raw bytes, or None if it can't be read."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            return hashlib.sha256(f.read()).hexdigest()
    except OSError:
        return None


def _bin_owner_ok(path):
    """True iff `path` is a REGULAR file, owned root:root, with no group/other write bit —
    the same shape as _extra_signers_owner_ok below, in its own function so a test (whose
    stub binaries are never really root-owned, since the test itself doesn't run as root)
    can monkeypatch it directly instead of faking filesystem ownership."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return False
    return st.st_uid == 0 and st.st_gid == 0 and not (st.st_mode & 0o022)


def _bin_dir_ok(d):
    """True iff `d`'s REAL directory — os.path.realpath, so a usrmerge symlink like
    /bin -> /usr/bin is followed to the directory that actually holds the files — is owned
    by uid 0 and not group/other-writable (v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109). A per-FILE check alone
    (_bin_owner_ok, above) trusts whatever directory a candidate binary happens to sit in;
    this closes that gap one level up, before any file inside the directory is even looked
    at. In its own monkeypatchable function for the same reason _bin_owner_ok is."""
    try:
        st = os.stat(os.path.realpath(d))
    except OSError:
        return False
    return stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode) and st.st_uid == 0 and not (st.st_mode & 0o022)


def _find_bin(name):
    """The first candidate under _BIN_DIRS (that fixed order) whose directory passes
    _bin_dir_ok AND is itself a regular file, root-owned, and not group/other-writable —
    every binary this helper ever runs is resolved this way, NEVER through $PATH
    (v5.66.0-beta.2, Q104; the directory-level check is v5.66.0-beta.7, Q109)."""
    for d in _BIN_DIRS:
        if not _bin_dir_ok(d):
            continue
        cand = os.path.join(d, name)
        if _bin_owner_ok(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
            return cand
    return None


def _run_bin(name, args, **kw):
    """Resolve `name` through _find_bin and run it with the fixed _CLEAN_ENV — never the
    inherited environment, never $PATH. `args` is the argument list AFTER the binary name.
    Returns a subprocess.CompletedProcess, or None if the binary could not be found at all
    under _BIN_DIRS (the caller decides what that means for its own op)."""
    binary = _find_bin(name)
    if binary is None:
        return None
    return subprocess.run([binary, *args], env=_CLEAN_ENV, **kw)  # nosec B603


def _unit_account(service):
    """The account the daemon's systemd unit runs as (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141; groups since build 11, Q148): None when the unit sets no
    `User=` or names root; else `{"name", "uid", "gid", "extra_groups"}`, where `gid` is the unit's `Group=` when it sets one (else the
    account's passwd primary group) and `extra_groups` are the account's /etc/group memberships (`os.getgrouplist`) plus the unit's
    `SupplementaryGroups=`, as gids - exactly the identity systemd gives the daemon, so what `-t` can read is what the daemon can read.
    `User=` may be a name or a numeric uid (build 12, Q149). A user or group the unit names that this host does not have is
    `{"refused": reason}`, never a guess and never root. Never raises: a host without systemd, `pwd` or `grp` simply has no answer."""
    unit = _resolve_unit(service, "status")
    if not unit:
        return None
    proc = _run_bin(
        "systemctl",
        ["show", "-p", "User", "-p", "Group", "-p", "SupplementaryGroups", unit],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )
    props = {}
    for line in (proc.stdout or "").splitlines() if proc else []:
        key, sep, value = line.partition("=")
        if sep:
            props[key.strip()] = value.strip()
    name = props.get("User", "")
    if not name or name == "root" or (name.isdigit() and int(name) == 0):
        return None  # no User=, or root by name or by uid: the daemon is root's, so the check is
    try:
        import grp
        import pwd
    except ImportError:  # not a POSIX host (only ever the test harness)
        return None
    try:
        pw = pwd.getpwuid(int(name)) if name.isdigit() else pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return {"refused": f"its unit runs as User={name}, which is not an account on this host"}
    if pw.pw_uid == 0:
        return None

    def gid_of(group):
        # `Group=` / `SupplementaryGroups=` take a name or a numeric gid
        return int(group) if group.isdigit() else grp.getgrnam(group).gr_gid

    gid, extra = pw.pw_gid, []
    try:
        if props.get("Group"):
            gid = gid_of(props["Group"])
        extra = [gid_of(g) for g in props.get("SupplementaryGroups", "").split() if g]
    except KeyError as e:
        return {"refused": f"its unit names the group {e.args[0]!r}, which does not exist on this host"}
    return {"name": pw.pw_name, "uid": pw.pw_uid, "gid": gid, "extra_groups": _with_memberships(pw.pw_name, gid, extra)}


def _with_memberships(name, gid, extra):
    """`extra` plus the account's own /etc/group memberships, first-seen order, the primary `gid` left out (the call sets it separately).
    systemd initialises a daemon's groups with initgroups(3) and then adds `SupplementaryGroups=`; `os.getgrouplist` is initgroups' list."""
    try:
        member = [g for g in os.getgrouplist(name, gid) if g != gid]
    except (OSError, AttributeError):
        member = []
    seen, out = set(), []
    for g in [*member, *extra]:
        if g not in seen and g != gid:
            seen.add(g)
            out.append(g)
    return out


def _daemon_bin_ok(path, uid):
    """True iff `path` is a REGULAR file owned by `uid`, a SYSTEM account (a uid below 1000, never
    root), with no group/other write bit - the only shape of Kea daemon binary the helper will run
    as that account (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141). In its own function so a test, whose stub binaries are
    never really owned by a system account, can monkeypatch it the way _bin_owner_ok is."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return False
    # S_IXUSR (build 13, Q150): the account that owns it is the one that executes it, and `os.access(X_OK)` is root's answer - true
    # when any class may execute - so an owner-without-u+x file with o+x passed and then failed to exec as that account
    return st.st_uid == uid and 0 < uid < 1000 and bool(st.st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR)


def _refusal_reason(path, unit_account):
    """Why a candidate that is not root:root is not trusted to run as the daemon's user - one
    sentence for the `detail` of `missingbinary` (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141). Names facts, never paths
    other than the binary's own."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return "cannot be read"
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return "is not a regular file"
    if st.st_mode & 0o022:
        return "is writable by its group or by everyone"
    if 0 < st.st_uid < 1000 and not st.st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR:
        return "is owned by a system account that has no execute permission on it"
    try:
        import pwd

        owner = pwd.getpwuid(st.st_uid).pw_name
    except (ImportError, KeyError):
        owner = f"uid {st.st_uid}"
    if st.st_uid == 0:
        return "is owned by root but not root:root, and the helper runs it as the daemon's user only if that user owns it"
    if st.st_uid >= 1000:
        return f"is owned by {owner}, not a system account and not the daemon's user"
    if unit_account is not None and "name" in unit_account:
        return f"is owned by {owner}, not the daemon's user {unit_account['name']}"
    return f"is owned by {owner}, which cannot be used as the daemon's user"


def _find_kea(service):
    """Resolve the Kea daemon binary and WHO runs it (v5.68.0-beta.6, Q141; identity first since build 12, Q149). Returns None when no
    candidate exists under _BIN_DIRS at all, else a dict: {"binary", "user", "group", "extra_groups"} on success - user/group None means
    "run as root" - or {"refused": reason} when a candidate exists but cannot be run safely as the daemon's own identity.

    THE ORDER IS THE POINT. (1) The unit's identity is resolved first (`_unit_account`: User= by name or uid, Group=,
    SupplementaryGroups= plus the account's /etc/group memberships); a user or group the host cannot resolve is a refusal, never root.
    (2) The binary is verified second: root:root with no group/other write bit (any unit), or owned by exactly the unit's account.
    (3) `-t` runs as the unit's identity whenever the unit names one - a root-owned binary under `User=_kea` runs as `_kea`, not root
    (build 11 ran it as root, because the unit was consulted only after the root:root check had already succeeded). Root runs `-t`
    only when the unit names no user, or root. With no unit user, the pre-5.68 rule holds: a root:root binary runs as root, a binary
    owned by a system account runs as that account. Candidates are looked at in _BIN_DIRS order in directories that pass
    _bin_dir_ok; the first trusted one wins."""
    name = _SERVICE_BINARY.get(service, "kea-dhcp6")
    refused = None
    unit_account = None
    unit_looked = False
    for d in _BIN_DIRS:
        if not _bin_dir_ok(d):
            continue
        cand = os.path.join(d, name)
        if not os.path.lexists(cand):
            continue
        if not unit_looked:
            unit_account, unit_looked = _unit_account(service), True
        if unit_account is not None and "refused" in unit_account:
            return {"refused": unit_account["refused"]}  # a user or group that does not exist is a refusal with its reason, not root
        root_owned = _bin_owner_ok(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK)
        if root_owned and unit_account is None:
            # run as root: the owner (root) bit is the one that will be used (build 13)
            if os.stat(cand).st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR:
                return {"binary": cand, "user": None, "group": None, "extra_groups": []}
            if refused is None:
                refused = "is root-owned and has no owner execute permission"
            continue
        if root_owned:
            # a root-owned binary under a unit that names an account: validate as that account (it must be able to execute the file)
            if os.stat(cand).st_mode & 0o001 or _group_may_exec(cand, unit_account):
                return {
                    "binary": cand,
                    "user": unit_account["uid"],
                    "group": unit_account["gid"],
                    "extra_groups": unit_account["extra_groups"],
                }
            if refused is None:
                refused = f"is root-owned and its mode does not let the daemon's user {unit_account['name']} execute it"
            continue
        try:
            import pwd

            owner = pwd.getpwuid(os.lstat(cand).st_uid)
        except (ImportError, KeyError, OSError):
            owner = None
        # who the daemon runs as: its unit's User=/Group=/SupplementaryGroups=, else the file's own owner and its primary group
        if unit_account is not None:
            run_uid, run_gid, run_extra = unit_account["uid"], unit_account["gid"], unit_account["extra_groups"]
        else:
            run_uid, run_gid, run_extra = None, None, []
        if run_uid is None and owner is not None:
            run_uid, run_gid = owner.pw_uid, owner.pw_gid
            run_extra = _with_memberships(owner.pw_name, run_gid, [])
        if run_uid is not None and _daemon_bin_ok(cand, run_uid) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
            return {"binary": cand, "user": run_uid, "group": run_gid, "extra_groups": run_extra}
        if refused is None:
            refused = _refusal_reason(cand, unit_account)
    return {"refused": refused} if refused is not None else None


def _group_may_exec(path, unit_account):
    """True iff `path`'s group has the execute bit and the unit's identity is in that group (its primary, `Group=` or supplementary
    ones). A root:root 0750 binary the daemon's account cannot execute would otherwise make `-t` die with a bare PermissionError."""
    try:
        st = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return bool(st.st_mode & 0o010) and st.st_gid in {unit_account["gid"], *unit_account["extra_groups"]}


def _kea_binary(service):
    """Absolute path to the kea-dhcp4/6/dhcp-ddns binary a root-run or daemon-run test would use, or
    None - kept for callers that only need the path; the run itself goes through _find_kea."""
    found = _find_kea(service)
    return found.get("binary") if found else None


# ── private files and the per-path lock (build 13, Q150) ─────────────────────


def _private_tempfile(directory, prefix):
    """(fd, path): a NEW file in `directory` - the target's own directory - with a unique name, created `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW`
    with mode 0600 whatever the umask (it can only remove bits), opened for writing. Every file this helper writes is created here, so a
    secret is private from its first byte and two helper processes never share a path. The caller owns the file and removes it on failure."""
    for _ in range(64):
        path = os.path.join(directory, f".{prefix}.{secrets.token_hex(8)}.jen_tmp")
        try:
            fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0), 0o600)
        except FileExistsError:
            continue
        return fd, path
    raise FileExistsError(f"no unused temp name in {directory}")


def _write_all(fd, data):
    view = memoryview(data)
    while view:
        view = view[os.write(fd, view) :]
    os.fsync(fd)


def _finish_private(fd, uid=None, gid=None, mode=0o600, path=None):
    """Give the DESCRIPTOR its final owner/group and mode, then close it. `uid`/`gid` None leave that part alone. A requested ownership that
    cannot be applied ABORTS (build 14, Q151): it used to be swallowed "for a test run as an ordinary user" and the caller then replaced the live file
    and reported success - a config or key owned by the wrong account. The caller removes its temp and leaves the live file untouched. `path` is only
    the fallback for a platform with no `fchmod` (the Windows dev box that imports this file); the helper itself runs on Linux."""
    try:
        if uid is not None or gid is not None:
            os.fchown(fd, -1 if uid is None else uid, -1 if gid is None else gid)
        if hasattr(os, "fchmod"):
            os.fchmod(fd, mode)
        elif path is not None:
            os.chmod(path, mode)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _install_private(path, data, uid=None, gid=None, mode=0o600):
    """Write `data` (bytes) to `path` atomically and private from the first byte: unique O_EXCL 0600 temp in the same directory, written
    and fsynced, final owner/group/mode applied to the descriptor, `os.replace`. Removes the temp on any failure and re-raises."""
    fd, tmp = _private_tempfile(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", os.path.basename(path))
    try:
        try:
            _write_all(fd, data)
        except BaseException:
            os.close(fd)
            raise
        _finish_private(fd, uid, gid, mode, tmp)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.unlink(tmp)
        raise


def _fsync_dir(directory):
    """fsync a directory so a rename in it is durable. The one expected exception - a filesystem that cannot fsync a directory descriptor
    (EINVAL / ENOTSUP / EBADF) - is named by errno; every other failure propagates (it is never `except OSError: pass`)."""
    if os.name == "nt":  # the Windows dev box that imports this file for its tests: a directory cannot be opened, nothing to sync
        return
    fd = os.open(directory, os.O_RDONLY)
    try:
        try:
            os.fsync(fd)
        except OSError as e:
            if e.errno not in (errno.EINVAL, errno.ENOTSUP, errno.EBADF):
                raise
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def _commit_file_set(members):
    """Install a SET of files all-or-nothing (build 14, Q151). `members` = [(path, data: bytes, uid, gid, mode)] in install order.

    1. STAGE every member: a unique private temp in its own directory (`_private_tempfile`, 0600 from the first byte), written, fsynced, given its
       final owner/group/mode on the descriptor. A failure here has touched no live file.
    2. SNAPSHOT every live member into memory WITHOUT moving it (the old design moved the live file aside before its replacement existed).
    3. REPLACE each staged file over its live path, in order.
    On ANY failure every member already replaced is put back byte-for-byte with its old owner and mode (one that did not exist is removed), every
    unreplaced temp is removed, and the original error is re-raised - or, if a restore ALSO failed, an OSError naming every path that is now wrong.
    A symlink at a live path is refused up front."""
    staged, snapshots, replaced = [], {}, []
    try:
        for path, data, uid, gid, mode in members:
            if os.path.islink(path):
                raise OSError(errno.ELOOP, "refusing to replace a symlink", path)
            fd, tmp = _private_tempfile(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", os.path.basename(path))
            staged.append((path, tmp))
            try:
                _write_all(fd, data)
            except BaseException:
                os.close(fd)
                raise
            _finish_private(fd, uid, gid, mode, tmp)
        for path, _tmp in staged:
            try:
                with open(path, "rb") as f:
                    st = os.fstat(f.fileno())
                    snapshots[path] = (f.read(), st.st_uid, st.st_gid, stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode))
            except FileNotFoundError:
                snapshots[path] = None
        for path, tmp in staged:
            os.replace(tmp, path)
            replaced.append(path)
    except BaseException as original:
        wrong = []
        for path in reversed(replaced):
            snap = snapshots.get(path)
            try:
                if snap is None:
                    os.unlink(path)
                else:
                    _install_private(path, snap[0], snap[1], snap[2], snap[3])
            except OSError as e:
                wrong.append(f"{path} ({e})")
        for _path, tmp in staged:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.unlink(tmp)
        if wrong:
            raise OSError(0, "the set could not be put back; these paths are now wrong: " + ", ".join(wrong)) from original
        raise
    for _path, tmp in staged:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.unlink(tmp)  # already renamed away on success: nothing left to remove


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _locked(path):
    """Hold an exclusive `flock` on `<path>.jen_lock` (created 0600) for the whole operation on `path`. EVERY test-config, apply-config,
    remove-config and install-tls takes it (build 13): it used to be taken only when an `expect_sha256` was given, so a validation never
    waited for an apply of the same file. A held lock is released when the descriptor closes, including when this process dies."""
    try:
        import fcntl  # Linux-only: the helper only ever runs there
    except ImportError:  # the Windows dev box that imports this file for its tests: no flock, so no lock
        yield
        return
    fd = os.open(path + ".jen_lock", os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR | getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0), 0o600)
    try:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.fchmod(fd, 0o600)
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        yield
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


# ── Kea config test (shared by test-config and apply-config) ─────────────────


def _run_kea_test(service, path, config, tls_paths):
    """Validate `config` with `kea-dhcpX -t` AS THE ACCOUNT THE DAEMON RUNS AS (`_find_kea`: its user, its Group= and its
    SupplementaryGroups=; root only for a root:root binary run as root) and ALWAYS remove the copy. The copy is a unique file in the
    config's own directory made by `_private_tempfile` (0600 from the first byte - it carries the database credentials), and just before
    the check it becomes `root:<the daemon's effective gid>` 0640 (build 13, Q150): READABLE by the account that runs `-t`, not WRITABLE by
    it, so that account cannot change the config between its write and its validation (it used to own the file). A root-run check keeps a
    root 0600 copy. The caller holds `_locked(path)`. Returns (ok: bool, result: dict). This only validates - apply re-writes its own."""
    found = _find_kea(service)
    name = _SERVICE_BINARY.get(service, "kea-dhcp6")
    if found is None or "binary" not in found:
        result = {"ok": False, "error": "missingbinary", "binary": name}
        if found and found.get("refused"):
            result["detail"] = found["refused"]
        return False, result
    binary = found["binary"]

    fd, tmp = _private_tempfile(os.path.dirname(path) or ".", os.path.basename(path))
    try:
        try:
            _write_all(fd, json.dumps(config, indent=2).encode("utf-8"))
        except BaseException:
            os.close(fd)
            raise
        if found.get("user") is not None:
            _finish_private(fd, 0, found["group"] if found.get("group") is not None else 0, 0o640, tmp)
        else:
            _finish_private(fd, None, None, 0o600, tmp)

        for entry in tls_paths or []:
            tp, kind = entry[0], (entry[1] if len(entry) > 1 else "file")
            present = os.path.isdir(tp) if kind == "dir" else os.path.isfile(tp)
            if not present:
                return False, {"ok": False, "error": "tlsmissing", "path": tp}

        kw = {}
        if found.get("user") is not None:
            # the daemon's own identity - account, Group= and SupplementaryGroups= - what `kea-dhcpX` itself runs as on every start
            kw = {"user": found["user"], "group": found["group"], "extra_groups": list(found.get("extra_groups") or [])}
        proc = subprocess.run([binary, "-t", tmp], capture_output=True, text=True, env=_CLEAN_ENV, **kw)  # nosec B603
        combined = (proc.stdout or "") + (proc.stderr or "")
        if proc.returncode != 0 or "ERROR" in combined:
            lines = [ln for ln in combined.splitlines() if "ERROR" in ln or "Error" in ln]
            return False, {"ok": False, "error": "testerror", "detail": " | ".join(lines[:3])}
        return True, {"ok": True}
    finally:
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.unlink(tmp)


# ── Ops ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def op_version(_payload):
    v = sys.version_info
    return {
        "ok": True,
        "helper_version": HELPER_VERSION,
        "helper_build": HELPER_BUILD,
        "python": f"{v.major}.{v.minor}.{v.micro}",
    }, None


def op_read_config(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    path = payload.get("path")
    if not _valid_service(service) or not _allowed_conf_path(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "missing"}, path
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            raw = f.read()
        return {"ok": True, "config": json.loads(raw), "sha256": hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()}, path
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "invalid-json", "detail": str(e)}, path


def op_test_config(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    path = payload.get("path")
    if not _valid_service(service) or not _allowed_conf_path(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    config = payload.get("config")
    if not isinstance(config, dict):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, path
    with _locked(path):  # build 13: a validation never races an apply (or another validation) of the same file
        _ok, result = _run_kea_test(service, path, config, payload.get("tls_paths") or [])
    return result, path


def op_apply_config(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    path = payload.get("path")
    if not _valid_service(service) or not _allowed_conf_path(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    config = payload.get("config")
    if not isinstance(config, dict):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, path
    allow_overwrite = bool(payload.get("allow_overwrite", True))
    # None → no concurrency guard (v1 callers). "" → "must not exist".
    # "<hex>" → the file must currently hash to exactly this.
    expect = payload.get("expect_sha256")

    with _locked(path):  # held until the end; taken for EVERY apply since build 13, `expect` or not
        if expect is not None:
            current = _file_sha256(path)  # None when the file is absent
            conflict = (current is not None) if expect == "" else (current != expect)
            if conflict:
                return {"ok": False, "error": "conflict", "sha256": current or ""}, path

        ok, result = _run_kea_test(service, path, config, payload.get("tls_paths") or [])
        if not ok:
            return result, path

        exists = os.path.exists(path)
        if exists and not allow_overwrite:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "exists"}, path

        data = json.dumps(config, indent=2).encode("utf-8")
        backup = None
        if exists:
            backup = path + ".jen_backup"
            st = os.stat(path)
            with open(path, "rb") as f:
                previous = f.read()
            # the backup is the whole previous config (credentials too): private from its first byte, the same owner and mode as it had
            _install_private(backup, previous, st.st_uid, st.st_gid, st.st_mode & 0o777)
            # the replacement keeps the destination's owner and mode, and is 0600 until it is complete (build 13)
            _install_private(path, data, st.st_uid, st.st_gid, st.st_mode & 0o777)
        else:
            # A brand-new config (build 14, Q151): it carries `lease-database.password`, so never 0644. The identity is the unit's own
            # (`_unit_account`, the one resolver): root:<the daemon's effective gid> 0640 when the unit names a non-root account (it can read
            # the file, and nobody else can), root:root 0600 when the daemon runs as root. Reached only once the file is complete.
            account = _unit_account(service)
            if account is not None and "refused" in account:
                return {"ok": False, "error": "unit-account", "detail": account["refused"]}, path
            if account is None:
                _install_private(path, data, 0, 0, 0o600)
            else:
                _install_private(path, data, 0, account["gid"], 0o640)
        return {"ok": True, "backup": backup, "sha256": _file_sha256(path)}, path


def op_remove_config(payload):
    """Remove a config file only if it still is the one the caller says (build 13, Q150): the rollback of an Author Kea Config target that
    had no file before. `expect_sha256` is required (never "" or None: there is no such thing as removing 'whatever is there'); the
    comparison and the unlink happen under the same lock an apply takes, so a file somebody replaced in between is left alone."""
    service = payload.get("service")
    path = payload.get("path")
    if not _valid_service(service) or not _allowed_conf_path(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    expect = payload.get("expect_sha256")
    if not isinstance(expect, str) or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{64}", expect):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, path
    with _locked(path):
        current = _file_sha256(path)
        if current is None:
            return {"ok": True, "removed": False}, path  # already gone: the state wanted
        if current != expect:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "conflict", "sha256": current}, path
        os.unlink(path)
        return {"ok": True, "removed": True}, path


def _resolve_unit(service, action):
    """The installed unit name for `service` — `kea-{fam}-server` on ISC
    packages, `isc-kea-{fam}-server` on older Debian/Ubuntu ones (`fam` is
    "dhcp4"/"dhcp6"/"dhcp-ddns" — Kea's own unit-naming family, not Jen's
    "d2" service name). Tries each with `systemctl show -p LoadState`;
    returns the first that is `loaded`, else None (including "systemctl
    itself could not be found under _BIN_DIRS", the old OSError-catch
    case)."""
    fam = _SERVICE_UNIT_FAM.get(service, "dhcp6")
    for unit in (f"kea-{fam}-server", f"isc-kea-{fam}-server"):
        proc = _run_bin("systemctl", ["show", "-p", "LoadState", "--value", unit], capture_output=True, text=True)
        if proc is None:
            return None
        if proc.stdout.strip() == "loaded":
            return unit
    return None


def _unit_state(unit):
    proc = _run_bin("systemctl", ["is-active", unit], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if proc is None:
        return "unknown"
    return proc.stdout.strip() or "unknown"


def op_service(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    action = payload.get("action")
    if not _valid_service(service) or action not in ("restart", "enable", "disable", "status"):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None

    unit = _resolve_unit(service, action)
    if unit is None:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "no-unit"}, None

    if action == "status":
        return {"ok": True, "unit": unit, "state": _unit_state(unit)}, unit

    args = [action]
    if action in ("enable", "disable"):
        args.append("--now")
    args.append(unit)
    proc = _run_bin("systemctl", args, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if proc is None:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "systemctl", "detail": "systemctl not found under _BIN_DIRS"}, unit
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        tail = "\n".join((proc.stderr or proc.stdout or "").splitlines()[-5:])
        return {"ok": False, "error": "systemctl", "detail": tail}, unit
    return {"ok": True, "unit": unit, "state": _unit_state(unit)}, unit


def op_tail_log(payload):
    path = payload.get("path")
    if not _allowed_log_path(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    try:
        lines = int(payload.get("lines", 200))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        lines = 200
    lines = max(1, min(lines, 1000))
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "missing"}, path
    try:
        # Bounded memory: only the last `lines` lines are ever held, decoded
        # per line with errors="replace", however large the log file is.
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            content = [ln.rstrip("\r\n") for ln in collections.deque(f, maxlen=lines)]
    except OSError as e:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "unreadable", "detail": str(e)}, path
    return {"ok": True, "lines": content}, path


def op_install_package(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    if not _valid_service(service):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    package = f"kea-{_SERVICE_UNIT_FAM.get(service, 'dhcp6')}-server"
    apt_env = {**_CLEAN_ENV, "DEBIAN_FRONTEND": "noninteractive"}
    out = []
    ok = True
    for args in (["update", "-qq"], ["install", "-y", package]):
        binary = _find_bin("apt-get")
        if binary is None:
            return {"ok": False, "output": "apt-get not found under _BIN_DIRS"}, package
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run([binary, *args], capture_output=True, text=True, env=apt_env)  # nosec B603
        except OSError as e:
            return {"ok": False, "output": str(e)}, package
        out.append((proc.stdout or "") + (proc.stderr or ""))
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            ok = False
            break
    tail = "\n".join("".join(out).strip().splitlines()[-15:])
    return {"ok": ok, "output": tail}, package


def _pem_ok(name, body):
    """Marker-and-size guard only (see the v4 note in the module doc)."""
    if not isinstance(body, str) or not body or len(body) > _PEM_MAX:
        return False
    if name == "server.key":
        return bool(_PEM_KEY_RE.match(body)) and "CERTIFICATE" not in body
    return bool(_PEM_CERTS_RE.match(body)) and "PRIVATE KEY" not in body


def op_install_tls(payload):
    service = payload.get("service")
    files = payload.get("files")
    if not _valid_service(service) or not isinstance(files, dict):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    if set(files) != set(_TLS_FILES):
        return {"ok": False, "error": "not-allowed"}, None
    for name in _TLS_FILES:
        if not _pem_ok(name, files[name]):
            return {"ok": False, "error": "bad-pem", "file": name}, None

    target_dir = os.path.join(_TLS_ROOT, service)  # a real fs path (remote = Linux), not a validated one
    for p in (_TLS_ROOT, target_dir):
        if os.path.islink(p):
            return {"ok": False, "error": "symlink", "path": p}, p
    for name in _TLS_FILES:
        if os.path.islink(os.path.join(target_dir, name)):
            return {"ok": False, "error": "symlink", "path": os.path.join(target_dir, name)}, os.path.join(target_dir, name)

    # build 14 (Q151): the key's group is the one `_unit_account` gives (the effective gid after `Group=`), the same identity validation runs as;
    # a daemon that runs as root (no unit `User=`) gets root:root
    account = _unit_account(service)
    if account is not None and "refused" in account:
        return {"ok": False, "error": "unit-account", "detail": account["refused"]}, None
    gid = 0 if account is None else account["gid"]
    group = "root"
    if account is not None:
        try:
            import grp

            group = grp.getgrgid(gid).gr_name
        except (ImportError, KeyError):
            group = str(gid)

    os.makedirs(target_dir, mode=0o755, exist_ok=True)
    os.chown(_TLS_ROOT, 0, 0)  # build 14: never swallowed - root cannot chown its own directory is a real fault, not a skip
    os.chown(target_dir, 0, 0)
    os.chmod(_TLS_ROOT, 0o755)
    os.chmod(target_dir, 0o755)

    members = [
        (os.path.join(target_dir, name), files[name].encode("utf-8"), 0, gid, 0o640 if name == "server.key" else 0o644)
        for name in _TLS_FILES
    ]
    with _locked(target_dir):  # build 13: two installs for one service never interleave
        try:
            _commit_file_set(members)  # build 14: all three or none - a failure puts every replaced member back
        except OSError as e:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "write-failed", "path": target_dir, "detail": str(e)}, target_dir
    return {"ok": True, "paths": {name: os.path.join(target_dir, name) for name in _TLS_FILES}, "owner": f"root:{group}"}, target_dir


